{
  "id": "COMPOUND:16888 - PROTEIN:891",
  "verbalization": "Theasaponin E1 is implicated in the inhibition of the Inhibitor of nuclear factor kappa-B kinase subunit alpha through a shared target of K-252a, which is a well-characterized inhibitor of the NF-κB signaling pathway. This relationship is supported by three distinct pathways, all of which link Theasaponin E1 to K-252a, which in turn targets the same downstream effector. The high scores across these paths suggest a consistent and robust mechanistic connection, particularly through the shared inhibition of K-252a. The presence of multiple targets (TAO1, Carboxypeptidase D, and Placenta growth factor) further indicates that Theasaponin E1 may act through diverse but related pathways, potentially modulating different aspects of cellular signaling. The integration of these pathways underscores the role of K-252a as a central mediator in the inhibitory effect of Theasaponin E1 on the NF-κB pathway. The mechanism is supported by the presence of a well-documented mode of action, namely the inhibition of K-252a, which is a known inhibitor of the NF-κB signaling cascade.",
